CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ explicitly instantiated: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ extern template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
no body for callee
--
N5008 [temp.explicit]/12: `extern template int box<int>::put(short);'
suppresses only the implicit instantiation; the program's explicit
instantiation definition defines the same specialization.  The parser kept
such a declaration of a member function, which became a bodiless member
hiding the template ("no body for callee box<int>::put"); it is now
discarded, as for a free function.  This makes libstdc++'s
basic_ostream::_M_insert instantiate the real num_put, which runs over the
modelled "C"-locale facets (cpp11_ostream_integer_insertion_classic_locale).
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG
assertion.
